##! The accepted type set, as a trait rather than a comment.
##!
##! An opinionated service says which types it carries. Saying it in prose means nothing checks
##! it; saying it as a trait means a field the set does not cover will not compile, and the error
##! names the type.
##!
##! The set: signed and unsigned integers, `f32`/`f64`, `String`, `bool`, and three composed
##! forms — an optional, a list of one accepted type, and a dictionary keyed by `String` over
##! one accepted type. Nothing else, and in particular nothing nested arbitrarily deep, because
##! a fixed depth is what lets every body be one `from_slice` into a concrete type.

use std::collections::BTreeMap

use serde::Serialize
use serde::de::DeserializeOwned

## A dictionary. `BTreeMap` rather than `HashMap`: ordered output without a sort, and no hashing
## per lookup on keys that are short.
pub type Map<T> = BTreeMap<String, T>

## Implemented for exactly the accepted types. The supertraits are what every one of them has to
## provide for a body to be read and written; the trait itself adds nothing but membership.
pub trait Field: Serialize + DeserializeOwned

## Fourteen identical impls are what a macro is for, and Uredo has Rust's. The body of a
## `macro_rules!` is Rust source (§22.5), which is exactly right here: what it generates *is*
## Rust items. It could not generate Uredo syntax, which is the limit of this technique.
macro_rules! scalar_fields {
    ($($t:ty),* $(,)?) => { $(impl Field for $t {})* };
}

scalar_fields!(u8, u16, u32, u64, usize, i8, i16, i32, i64, isize, f32, f64, bool, String)

## The composed forms, which take a bound and so are ordinary generic impls rather than macro
## output. One level each, by design — `Vec<Vec<T>>` is accepted only because `Vec<T>` is itself
## a field, which is the deepest this goes without a schema.
impl<T: Field> Field for Vec<T>

impl<T: Field> Field for Map<T>

impl<T: Field> Field for T?
